feat(deploy): mint the CA root from a key that never enters the CA (#238)

Rungs 1 and 2 keep the CA key from being destroyed by accident. Neither changes
what an attacker who reads the CA's container gets, and that is the half this
issue exists for.

Under the key-custody overlay the key is created inside a secret store and is
reached over that store's API. Reading Fulcio's container now yields a TOKEN —
revocable, auditable, with an expiry — instead of a key, which is none of those
things. It is not "the CA cannot be abused"; it is "abuse has an expiry and a
log".

ca-bootstrap is its own program and its own image, not an innsegl subcommand,
for the reason innsegl.yml already gives about the pseudonymisation secret:
that would put certificate authority inside the binary holding SPIRE admin. It
speaks the store's HTTP API with the standard library rather than pulling a KMS
client tree into a repository with eleven direct dependencies, for two JSON
requests whose exact shape is measured in the tests beside it.

x509.CreateCertificate takes a crypto.Signer and never asks for a private key,
so the self-signed root is produced without the key ever being in the process.

MEASURED END TO END, not argued. Against a real store: the key was created, the
root minted, and Fulcio started with --ca=kmsca --kms-resource=hashivault://...
with no FATAL and served a trust bundle carrying that root.

That run also found a defect before it shipped. The overlay first left Fulcio
mounting the FILE CA's volume, which holds ca.key — a kmsca deployment with a
private key sitting beside it, and OPS-049's claim would have been false on any
existing deployment. The CA now mounts a volume holding a certificate and an
issuer config and nothing else; the config is COPIED there rather than mounted,
because the generated one lives next to ca.key. The merged configuration has
zero fileca flags and one volume.

The store starts SEALED and there is no unseal key in this repository. #238:
"A dev-mode store that unseals with a known key is theatre and must not be what
ships." So there is no server -dev, no root token in an environment variable,
and bring-up of the default stack is untouched — the overlay is a second -f and
nothing else reads it.

The confined write uses os.Root rather than a suppression: the repository has no
nosec precedent and this is not the file to start one in.

Driven by OPS-049, OPS-050 and OPS-052 at unit level; their live halves and the
operator flow are the rest of this issue.

